### How do you use a constructor reference in a stream?

If Employee has a constructor taking a String name, Employee::new serves as Function<String, Employee>.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class ConstructorRefDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> names = List.of("Rajesh", "Priya", "Amit");

        // Lambda
        List<Employee> lambda = names.stream()
            .map(name -> new Employee(name))
            .collect(Collectors.toList());

        // Constructor reference (equivalent)
        List<Employee> ref = names.stream()
            .map(Employee::new)
            .collect(Collectors.toList());

        ref.forEach(e -> System.out.println(e.getName()));
    }
}
```

```text
Rajesh
Priya
Amit
```

### How does the compiler select the right constructor overload?

The target functional interface determines which constructor overload is used.

| Target interface | Constructor matched |
|---|---|
| Function<String, Employee> | Employee(String name) |
| BiFunction<String, String, Employee> | Employee(String name, String dept) |
| Supplier<Employee> | Employee() (no-arg) |

```java
// Two-argument constructor
List<String[]> pairs = List.of(
    new String[]{"Rajesh", "Loans"},
    new String[]{"Priya", "IT"}
);

BiFunction<String, String, Employee> twoArg = Employee::new;
Employee e = twoArg.apply("Rajesh", "Loans");
```

### How do you use a constructor reference with toArray?

Stream.toArray() without arguments returns Object[]. Pass an array constructor reference for a typed array.

```java
Employee[] array = names.stream()
    .map(Employee::new)
    .toArray(Employee[]::new);

System.out.println(Arrays.toString(array));
```

### When should you keep the lambda instead?

If construction requires validation, defaulting, or extra logic, a bare constructor reference has no room for it.

```java
// Constructor reference: simple, no validation
.map(Employee::new)

// Lambda: needed for validation
.map(name -> {
    if (name == null || name.isBlank()) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Name required");
    }
    return new Employee(name.strip());
})

// Static factory method: encapsulates validation
.map(Employee::create)  // Employee.create(String) validates internally
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: ambiguous constructor overloads.

```java
// If Employee has Employee(String) and Employee(Object),
// and the target is Function<String, Employee>,
// the compiler may report ambiguity.
// Fix: use an explicit lambda to resolve.
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Constructor references pair naturally with map to turn raw data into domain objects.
- They assume the constructor alone fully and safely builds the object.
- When the construction needs validation, use a static factory method reference instead.
- toArray(Type[]::new) is the standard way to convert a stream to a typed array.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What is the difference between Employee::new and () -> new Employee()?" Both create employees, but the first is a constructor reference and the second is a lambda. They compile similarly.
- "Can you use constructor references with generics?" Yes: ArrayList::new creates ArrayList instances.
- "How do you handle checked exceptions in a constructor reference?" Wrap in a method that catches, or use a custom functional interface that declares the exception.
